# Privacy Policy — Day & Night Journal

_Last updated: October 12, 2026_

Day & Night Journal ("the app," "we," "us") is built to be private by design. This
policy explains exactly what the app does and does not do with your information.

## The short version

We do not have a server. We never receive, see, store, sell, share, mine, or use your
journal to train anything. Your data lives on your device and in **your own private
iCloud account** — nowhere else.

## Information the app uses

**Your journal entries.** Everything you type or select — prompts, notes, checklists,
moods, water counts, and any custom fields you create — is stored locally on your
device using Apple's on-device database, and synced across your own Apple devices
through your private iCloud (CloudKit) account. We have no access to this iCloud
container; only you do, through your Apple ID.

**Apple Health data (optional, read-only).** If you choose to enable it, the app reads
your sleep times, step count, exercise minutes, heart rate, resting heart rate,
workouts, time in daylight, date of birth, and heart rate variability (HRV) from Apple
Health. This access is **read-only** — the app never writes to Apple Health. What the
app keeps from those reads stays on your device or in your own private iCloud. Journal
fields filled from Health sync like anything else you enter. Protocols lines are worked
out on this device and kept on this device. None of this is sent to us, and it is
**never used for advertising, marketing, or data mining**. You can turn Health off at
any time in the iOS Settings app, and you can decline it entirely; the app works fully
without it.

**Apple Weather and approximate location (optional).** If you turn Protocols on and
allow location, the app sends an approximate location (rounded) to Apple Weather to get
cloud cover for the morning-light target. That location is not stored in your journal
and is not sent to us. With no location, or no answer from Apple Weather, the sky stays
unknown.

**Sign in with Apple.** The app uses Sign in with Apple solely to gate access to your
journal. We store only the anonymous, stable user identifier that Apple provides, on
your device. We do not collect your name or email, we operate no account server, and we
store no authentication tokens anywhere off your device.

**Notifications.** If you turn them on, the app schedules optional morning and evening
reminders to journal, and a weekly look back, on your device. These are local
notifications only — no push server is involved, their text is written on your device, and
no information leaves your device to deliver them.

**Evening voice notes (optional).** If you record a voice note, the audio and its
transcript are kept on your device. Transcription runs on your device. When iCloud is on,
the note also syncs to your other devices through your own private iCloud (a private
CloudKit zone that only your Apple ID can read); with iCloud off, it stays on this device
only. It never goes to a server of ours. Deleting a note deletes it everywhere it synced.

**Insights.** The Insights cards work out counts and averages from answers you have already
written — for example this month so far against last month, your mood by weekday, or how
often a habit was kept. It is arithmetic, worked out on your device, over your own journal.
These cards involve no language model, upload nothing, use no account, and ask for no
new permissions. It describes what happened on the same days — never why — and it is not
advice. (The optional "Patterns" card this paragraph used to describe was retired in
version 1.8, build 102.)

**Gain Insight (optional, version 2.0).** On devices that support Apple Intelligence, the
Insights tab can answer a question about your numbers. Nothing is sent until you tap Accept.
Your question and short summaries of your numbers (streaks, averages, counts, and answers from
the last few days) are given to Apple's on-device model. Paragraph answers, daily questions
and photos are never sent. A question too large for the on-device model may be sent to Apple
Private Cloud Compute, which Apple runs under its own privacy design
(https://security.apple.com/blog/private-cloud-compute/). It never goes to a server of ours or
to any other company's model. Your questions and answers are kept on your device only, and you
can delete them one at a time or all at once. The model cannot change your journal.

## What we do NOT collect

- No analytics, tracking, advertising identifiers, or third-party SDKs.
- No usage telemetry sent to us.
- No location is sent to us, and none is stored in your journal. An approximate location (rounded) may be sent to Apple Weather for cloud cover, as described above.
- Nothing transmitted to any server operated by us — because we operate none.

## Data sharing

We do not share your information with anyone, because we never have it. Your data syncs
only through Apple's iCloud under your own Apple ID, governed by
[Apple's Privacy Policy](https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/).

## Deleting your data

You are always in control:

- **Sign out** (in the app's About screen) returns you to the sign-in screen while
  leaving your journal safe in your iCloud.
- **Delete account & all data** (in the app's About screen) permanently erases every
  entry, custom field and voice note from your device and your iCloud. This cannot be undone.
- You can also delete the app and remove its iCloud data via iOS Settings → your name →
  iCloud → Manage Account Storage.

## Children

The app is not directed at children and collects no personal information for any user.

